{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>And you shall take for yourselves on the first day the fruit of a beautiful tree.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Vayikra Rabbah 30) that it is called 'first' for the reckoning of sins — a parable to a province that owes tribute to the king, etc.; thus on the eve of Rosh HaShanah the great ones went out to meet Him, etc., and during the Ten Days of Repentance the intermediate ones, and on Yom HaKippurim all go out, etc. And one must ponder: why did they call it 'the first day for the reckoning of sins' when He commanded concerning these four species? And why did they not call it 'the first day for the reckoning of sins' when He commanded concerning the sukkah and the festival? And what did the author of this midrash see to make three divisions corresponding to three classes, and what would have been lacking if he had said that it is therefore called 'the first day' because on Yom HaKippurim everything is forgiven, and between Yom HaKippurim and Sukkot they commit no sins — therefore it is called 'the first day for the reckoning of sins'?" "And it appears that the author of this midrash wished to resolve two difficulties: both the difficulty of 'is it then the first [day]?', and the difficulty of 'for yourselves (לָכֶם).' And he did not wish to explain 'for yourselves' as 'from your own' — to exclude stolen property, for he holds that this is not the implication of 'לָכֶם,' for 'from you (מִכֶּם)' and 'in you (בָּכֶם)' are opposites: thus 'from your own' would be מִכֶּם, whereas לָכֶם means בָּכֶם. Rather, surely these four species correspond to four classes of Israel: פְּרִי עֵץ הָדָר, which has taste and fragrance, corresponds to those possessors of Torah and mitzvah, and they are the perfectly righteous; עַרְבֵי נַחַל, which have neither taste nor fragrance, correspond to the classes that have neither Torah nor mitzvah, and they are the thoroughly wicked; כַּפּוֹת תְּמָרִים, which have food but no fragrance, and עֲנַף עֵץ עָבֹת, which has fragrance but no food — these two correspond to the intermediate ones who have either Torah without mitzvah or mitzvah without Torah. Therefore it says 'וּלְקַחְתֶּם לָכֶם,' that is, לָכֶם literally — just as we find בָּכֶם these three classes, so shall you take four species that correspond to these three classes. And alongside this division, Scripture informed us that this day is 'the first for the reckoning of sins.' If so, the question presses: what does this have to do with that? Rather, surely one depends on the other — because there are among you these three classes and they achieve atonement one by one, through this this day becomes 'the first for the reckoning.' And if the intent of Scripture were to suspend all atonement on Yom HaKippurim alone, what would emerge from it informing us at this point that Israel is divided into three classes? Rather, surely its intention is to say that Yom HaKippurim does not atone for all of them at once, but little by little the Holy One, blessed be He, releases first one, then the next, in the order recalled through these four species: first He absolves the sin of the class likened to פְּרִי עֵץ הָדָר, and they are the great ones who first went out to meet Him, blessed be He, on the eve of Rosh HaShanah; afterward He absolves a third on account of the intermediate ones who went out to meet Him during the Ten Days of Repentance, who are likened to כַּפּוֹת תְּמָרִים and עֲנַף עֵץ עָבֹת; and afterward, on Yom HaKippurim, He absolves them all, even the wicked who are likened to עַרְבֵי נַחַל, for then all went out to meet Him and fast. And therefore the Kohen Gadol was not permitted to enter the Holy of Holies on Yom HaKippurim except in a cloud of incense, for in it the chelbanah is mixed with the spices of the incense, to gather in all the transgressors of Israel and make them one bundle (אֲגֻדָּה אַחַת) with the good ones, so that these would atone for those — in the manner the midrash states concerning these four species: to destroy them is impossible, rather let them be made one bundle (אֲגֻדָּה אַחַת) and they atone for one another. And if one of these classes is missing, the Kohen Gadol has no entry to come into the Holy. And this is a very precious interpretation." "And the matter of repentance may also be included in this bundle (אֲגֻדָּה), for it is known that throughout all the days of the year the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts only the repentance of the many, but the repentance of the individual the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts only during the Ten Days of Repentance — as they derived (Rosh HaShanah 18a) from the verse ״דִּרְשׁוּ יְהֹוָה בְּהִמָּצְאוֹ״ (Isaiah 55:6). And therefore Scripture wished to give good reason and understanding why they must be one bundle (אֲגֻדָּה אַחַת) on this day more than on the preceding days. Therefore it comes as though responding and says: because it is 'the first for the reckoning of sins' — to tell you that it would surely have been fitting to make them one bundle (אֲגֻדָּה אַחַת) at the start of the year, so that they would remain so throughout all the days of the year, to prepare themselves for the repentance of all the days of the year should they sin, and then the penitents would be many and their repentance would be accepted throughout all the days of the year. But from Rosh HaShanah until Yom HaKippurim there is not so great a need to make them one bundle (אֲגֻדָּה אַחַת), for in those days even the repentance of the individual is accepted; and from Yom HaKippurim to Sukkot they are occupied with the mitzvah of sukkah and lulav and commit no sins, for 'a mitzvah at the time of one's engagement in it protects and saves from all sin' — and at this time too there is not so great a need for the repentance of the many. But on the first day of the festival, which is 'the first for the reckoning of sins,' therefore they must prepare on this very day for the repentance of the many — and that is when all four classes are in one bundle (אֲגֻדָּה אַחַת) and they atone for one another, and then their repentance is accepted throughout all the days of the year. And through this all the doubts are also resolved in a correct and clear manner." "<b>And you shall rejoice before YHWH your Elohim.</b> Their rejoicing is before YHWH only when they are in one bundle (אֲגֻדָּה אַחַת), and especially since the rejoicing is specifically on this festival, because it is the season of gathering, wherein it is the way of the world to rejoice especially. Therefore He warned them that the rejoicing should be before YHWH specifically — not like the joy of the empty ones, all whose devices are 'slaughtering cattle and killing sheep' (Isaiah 22:13), 'drinking wine in bowls,' and multiplying discord in Israel; and through such joy they destroyed both Houses of the Sanctuary and went forth at the head of the exiles, as it says (Isaiah 55:12): ״כִּי בְשִׂמְחָה תֵּצֵאוּ״. As our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Kiddushin 81a): the festival is an occasion of contention (סְקָבָא דְּשַׁתָּא רִגְלָא) — for because they rejoiced not for the sake of Heaven, they brought on every illness and every affliction, like the deed of the etrog that they pelted at King Yannai (see Sukkah 48b). And of such rejoicing Scripture says 'what does this accomplish?' — for through it you shall go forth at the head of the exiles. But ״בְּשָׁלוֹם״ — when they are in one bundle (אֲגֻדָּה אַחַת) and there is peace among them — ״תּוּבָלוּן״ unto the land; ״הֶהָרִים וְהַגְּבָעוֹת״ — these are the heads of the academy who are exalted and inspire awe (Ezekiel 1:18); ״יִפְצְחוּ לִפְנֵיכֶם רִנָּה״ — for they shall precede everything that pertains to sanctity according to their due rank, and the young men shall not shove the elders as in the affair of the spies. ״וְכָל עֲצֵי הַשָּׂדֶה יִמְחֲאוּ כָף״ — these are all four classes alluded to in these four species, which come from the trees of the field; they shall clap hands to rejoice before YHWH. And likewise in another place (Vayikra Rabbah 30:4) they expounded this verse ״אָז יְרַנְּנוּ כָל עֲצֵי יָעַר״ (Psalms 96:12) concerning these four species, and alluded in the word אָז — א׳ ז׳ [= one, seven] — that the lulav is taken in the Temple all seven days and in the provinces one day."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:38:40.890734698Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}